  "id": "Compound::DB00373 - Disease::DOID:3393",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Timolol may be linked to Coronary Artery Disease through a series of indirect pathways involving inflammation and cardiovascular effects. Timolol, an organic molecule, is implicated in causing Serositis, a disorder by site, which is in turn treated by Enalapril or Perindopril. These medications are known to influence cardiovascular health, and their interaction with Serositis may contribute to the development of Coronary Artery Disease. The shared lowest common ancestors—Organic Molecule and Disorder By Site—highlight a potential mechanistic link between Timolol and the disease, though the exact nature of this relationship remains unclear. The evidence supports a possible role of Timolol in exacerbating or contributing to Coronary Artery Disease through inflammatory pathways, but further investigation is needed to confirm the direct causal relationship.",
